This collection consists of digital reproductions of the photographs in the Robert J. Moore Scrapbooks at the University of Arizona Special Collections. The collection is a repository of images by Robert J. (Bob) Moore that relate to the history of New Mexico and southern Colorado.

Moore arrived in Gallup at age twenty-one in 1911 with ten dollars in his pocket, determined to become the best photographer in the southwest. Though his early subjects were not the cultural treasures that would make it famous throughout the United States -- though only thirty-four when he died -- Gallup nonetheless became a center for the industry and Moore was its most successful photographer. For twenty-nine years Moore captured countless images that have become iconic in this region -- including Native Americans dancing in the streets of Gallup; the railroad track laid across the Turrettes, making the first transcontinental journey; Native Americans gathered with the U.S. Cavalry at Old Crow; Indians riding horses under the great sky at Gallup; Indians gathering for ceremonies at San Ildefonso;
